{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["And He said, <b>Do not turn to the elilim</b> — this is from the language of 'and if your heart turns' (Deuteronomy 30:17), 'whose heart turns away today' (Deuteronomy 29:17). It means that one's heart should not turn toward the elilim to believe that there is benefit in them, or that future events will come about which they prophesy from them; rather, they and all their doings should be nothing and void in one's eyes, and the future will come only by decree of the Most High. And thus our Rabbis said (Shabbat 149a): it is forbidden even to look at their images themselves, because it says 'Do not turn to the elilim.' They included even gazing at them within the prohibition, so that one should not give any thought to their affairs whatsoever. And He said, <b>and elohim of cast metal you shall not make for yourselves</b>, to warn them concerning the act of making them. And the warnings concerning idolatry appear in the Torah in many places. And in the midrash of Vayikra Rabbah (24:5) they commented on this passage: Rabbi Levi says, because the Ten Commandments are included within it — 'I am YHWH your Elohim' (Exodus 20:2), and here it is written 'I am YHWH your Elohim'; 'You shall have no' (Exodus 20:3), and here it is written 'and elohim of cast metal you shall not make for yourselves'; 'You shall not take' (Exodus 20:7), and here it is written (Leviticus 19:12) 'and you shall not swear falsely by My name'; 'Remember the Sabbath day' (Exodus 20:8), and here it is written 'and you shall keep My sabbaths'; 'Honor your father and your mother' (Exodus 20:12), and here it is written 'You shall each fear his mother and his father'; 'You shall not murder' (Exodus 20:13), and here it is written (Leviticus 19:16) 'you shall not stand idly by the blood of your fellow'; 'You shall not commit adultery' (Exodus 20:13), and here it is written (Leviticus 19:29) 'Do not profane your daughter to make her a harlot'; 'You shall not steal' (Exodus 20:13), and here it is written (Leviticus 19:11) 'you shall not steal'; 'You shall not testify falsely' (Exodus 20:13), and here it is written (Leviticus 19:16) 'you shall not go about as a talebearer among your people'; 'You shall not covet' (Exodus 20:14), and here it is written 'and you shall love your fellow as yourself' (Leviticus 19:18) — thus far in the midrash."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "I have rendered האלילים as 'the elilim' to preserve the Hebrew term Ramban glosses, which is distinct from the divine name אלהים appearing later in the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:54.666274833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}